  "sections": {
    "uses": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Ficus sur § Uses",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ficus_sur#Uses",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
        "revision": 1373786744,
        "heading": "Uses",
        "text": "The figs are edible and utilized in fresh or dried form by native people in many regions. They are also suited to preparation of fig preserve, if other suitable fruit are added.\nThe heavily clustered figs suggest fecundity, and some trees in East Africa have been venerated as sacred shrines in animist practices.\nThe wood is light and soft, and is not much used commercially.\n\n■ Traditional medicinal plant\nAll parts may exude a latex, which has some traditional medicinal plant uses. The latex has been shown to contain ursene and oleanane triterpenoids, of which the latter may be effective in cancer treatment, while a methanolic extract from the roots is potentially effective against chloroquine-resistant malaria.",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "es": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (es): Ficus sur § Usos",
